# Media
# Wallpapers and audio are large binaries that are either generated from
# `infrastructure/scripts/` or licensed separately, so they are reproducible
# rather than authored here — git history is the wrong place for a gigabyte of
# 4K JPEGs. `media/` is purely what the API serves locally while R2 is
# unconfigured, so nothing in it is source at all.
media/
media-src/**/*.jpg
media-src/**/*.jpeg
media-src/**/*.png
media-src/**/*.webp
media-src/**/*.mp3
media-src/**/*.m4a
media-src/**/_derivatives/

# The manifest is the opposite: it is the licence record for the set, it is
# small, and losing it would leave a directory of images nobody can prove the
# rights to. It stays tracked.
!media-src/**/manifest.json
